  "summary": "Malicious code in testing1345 (PyPI)",
  "details": "\n---\n_-= Per source details. Do not edit below this line.=-_\n\n## Source: amazon-inspector (89784543a54361ccd20f7d5986a3468bfb12998dfd283b6737f9d9b1ad8c4555)\nThe sdist's setup.py registers a custom build_py command that invokes testing1345.hello.fetch(), which downloads https://helper-exe.vercel.app/hello.bat to %TEMP% and executes it via cmd.exe. The bdist_wheel override additionally writes a site-packages.pth file (testing1345_boot.pth) containing 'import testing1345_boot'; that module calls the same fetch() on Windows at interpreter startup, repeating the download-and-run of hello.bat from the unpinned third-party Vercel host with no hash or signature verification. A temp-file marker gates the boot path to once per machine but is user-deletable. A separate vmcheck module reads DMI/SMBIOS strings and Hyper-V registry keys to classify the host as physical or virtual; it is exposed as a library API and not wired into the install/boot fetch path, but commonly accompanies dropper behavior.\n\n## Source: kam193 (15bab830cd8343859ac220ef87e821719169a4e2ccb50da37032d97ef02c27dd)\nDuring installation, the package downloads and executes a script from a remote location. The server delivers different payload versions, some of them being benign, and others containing next-stage installation. The final payload is a RAT.\n\n\n---\n\nCategory: MALICIOUS - The campaign has clearly malicious intent, like infostealers.\n\n\nCampaign: 2026-11-testing1345\n\n\nReasons (based on the campaign):\n\n\n - Downloads and executes a remote malicious script.\n\n\n - The package overrides the install command in setup.py to execute malicious code during installation.\n\n\n - rat\n",
